          "He answered it the way he always did: quietly, sincerely, half a second behind the muezzin’s cadence, as though terrified of interrupting. It was the only part of his morning he refused to rush. For those three minutes, the chaotic clutter of his thoughts went completely still.",
          "(The narrator strongly recommends savouring this moment. It is the calmest Ma’un will be all day.)",
          "He walked back from the mosque with the sunrise resting warm on his shoulders and the empty street smelling of damp asphalt. He felt light in a way he couldn't quite explain—thinking about nothing in particular, which, if he were entirely honest with himself, meant thinking about married life.",
          "Not the wedding. Weddings sounded like a logistical nightmare of rented suits, perfume headaches, and aunts giving unsolicited advice. No, he thought about the quiet reality that came after: someone to share a terrible joke with in the dark. Someone who knew without asking how many spoons of sugar went into his tea. Someone to stand beside him in prayer when the night grew cold. And maybe a two-slice toaster. He had surprisingly strong feelings about toasters.",
          "He was still privately debating the merits of bagel settings when he pushed open the front door and stepped into the kitchen.",
