          "When a man returns to the city that swallowed his entire life, he moves like an unwelcome shadow. The train\n      clattered over the steel bridges of northern Tokyo, the cold river waters rushing gray and swollen beneath the\n      trestles. I carried the canvas rucksack between my boots. Inside it, money sat bundled in brown paper—eighteen\n      million yen that had been spat upon in Shizuoka, money that carried the scent of wet soot, cold steel, and\n      unhealed graves.",
          "The neighbors in our alley watched me from behind their drawn blinds when I walked up the lane. I was a ghoul to\n      them. A widower who had lost his daughter to a freight truck and his wife to the bathwater within the span of\n      three months. Nobody greeted me. When I passed the small noodle shop at the corner, the owner quietly pulled his\n      wooden menu board inside and slid the lattice door shut. Human beings naturally recoil from extreme tragedy; they\n      fear that bad luck is a contagious disease that can drift across the asphalt on a winter breeze.",
